This assigenment is for how to patch for file or open source packages (or ports in FreeBSD).

Similary, we can patch for open source packages (or ports in FreeBSD).
There could be sligthly difference in Ubuntu and FreeBSD. However,
most of the commands are identicals.

Ready Makefile should be there for open source packages or ports for
easy build, otherwise need to put efforts for creating Makefile.

Here, we are using 'patch' command for file patching. also, we have
used 'diff' command for support. and this is standard way to create
and build the patches.

detail steps:
------------------
1. go to location; i.e # cd <path/to/your/source/file>
2. take copy of that file i.e. cp sample.c sample.c.orig
3. edit file and save the file with new changes; i.e. vi sample.c
4. then using 'diff' create patch file i.e. diff -buN sample.c.orig sample.c > patch-sample.c
	*Note: patch file name can be anything.
5. Build or Apply the patch using 'patch' (or Makefile if available) i.e.  patch < patch-sample.c
	*Note: use option '-b' to take a backup before applying patch or for setting backup file version use '-V' option along with option '-b' i.e. # patch -b < patch-sample.c or # patch -b -V < patch-sample.c respectively..
6. try to dry-run for created patch before applying, because if some errors are there in newly created patch then it will be risky is some cases. i.e patch --dry-run < patch-sample.c
	*Note: Reverse/Undo a patch can be possible using # patch -R < patch-sample.c
7. Once dry-run succeeded then can build and install patch (using Makefile).


*Note:
1. Always create Makefile. And of course, for real time projects Makefile will be there.





